  "id": "Compound::DB00387 - Gene::1129",
  "verbalization": "Procyclidine is shown to interact with Chrm4 through binding, and Chrm4 is involved in multiple signaling pathways that ultimately lead to its participation in the Chrm2 activity. These pathways include G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor activity, muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, and adenylate cyclase-inhibiting signaling. The evidence suggests that Procyclidine may influence Chrm2 through these interconnected molecular mechanisms, potentially modulating its activity. The scores from the three paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, with the highest score in path_18966. The overlapping involvement of Chrm4 in multiple signaling pathways supports the idea that Procyclidine could exert its effects on Chrm2 via a shared regulatory network. The integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of the molecular interactions, emphasizing the role of G-protein coupled receptors in mediating the effects of Procyclidine on Chrm2.",
